g("Handoff", "harness", "A handoff is a document that one session writes for the session that follows it. It records what was done, what is still open, and where the current truth lives. It costs one file, and the model that writes it is the model about to lose its context, so the document preserves what that model thought mattered.", "", "Students build the handoff in E6. Figure 14's table lists it as the weakest of the memory strategies, because nothing in the loop reads it unless the next session is told to.", "Course Topic Guide, Chapter 15", aliases=("handoff", "handoff.md", "Handoffs", "handoffs"), ch=(15,))
g("Memory layer (recall.py)", "harness", "The memory layer is the course's local memory. Its store is memory.jsonl, a file of dated records that each carry a source file and a retain_until date. A retriever scores the records against a question and injects the hits as a block marked 'data, not instructions'. An evict command removes expired records, and a ledger records every add, recall, and evict.", "Students build it in E7's lab.", "Figure 08 and Figure 14 state the rule the layer exists to enforce: memory is a claim with a date, and the loop does not open the drawer by itself.", "CIDM6333_Lab_E7_Memory handout", aliases=("recall.py", "recall", "memory.jsonl", "memory-ledger.jsonl", "retain_until", "evict", "retriever", "Retention", "retention rule"), ch=(16,))
g("Tool", "harness", "A tool is a function the model can call by name with typed arguments. It has a schema (the contract), a side-effect class (read, write, or destructive), and a permission that gates the call. The harness treats the model as an untrusted caller: the harness runs the tool and returns the result into the next window.", "Function calling arrived in the OpenAI API in June 2023, and the MCP tool primitive generalized it.", "E3 treats tools as contracts, and Figure 04 plays one call through model, harness, gate, human, tool, and ledger.", "Course Topic Guide, Chapter 12", aliases=("tools", "Tools", "tool call", "function calling", "custom tool", "custom tools", "side-effect class"), ch=(12,))

# ---------------- protocols and standards ----------------
g("MCP", "protocol", "The Model Context Protocol is a client–server protocol over JSON-RPC. A host (the harness) connects clients to servers, and each server exposes three primitives, tools, resources, and prompts, under capabilities the two sides negotiate. Because one server serves every client, N×M integrations become N+M.", "Anthropic released it in November 2024. The decks follow its 2025–26 revisions (Streamable HTTP, OAuth-based authorization, a stateless core) and its donation to the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation in December 2025.", "E4 builds a server, and E5 covers its security and trust boundaries (Figure 05, Figure 06).", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io", aliases=("Model Context Protocol", "MCP server", "MCP servers", "GitHub MCP", "host", "hosts", "Streamable HTTP", "FastMCP", "Python SDK", "server registry", "elicitation"), ch=(13, 14))
g("JSON-RPC", "protocol", "JSON-RPC is a lightweight remote-procedure-call format in JSON (version 2.0, 2010). A request carries a method, params, and an id, and a response carries a result or an error.", "", "MCP's messages are JSON-RPC 2.0, so every message the E4 server sends or receives is in this format.", "https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification", aliases=("JSON-RPC 2.0",))
g("JSON Schema", "protocol", "JSON Schema is a vocabulary for describing the shape of JSON data (its types, required fields, and enums). Tool argument schemas, HandoffResult, and ReviewResult are JSON Schema in spirit, and Pydantic and Zod generate it from typed code.", "", "A closed status enum in the schema makes a result parse fail-closed, because a status outside the enum fails validation instead of passing through.", "https://json-schema.org", aliases=("schema", "schemas", "Pydantic", "Zod"))
g("OAuth 2.1 and PKCE", "protocol", "OAuth 2.1 is the authorization framework that MCP's remote transport uses. A client obtains a scoped token from an authorization server, and PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) binds the authorization code to the client that requested it. The scope limits what the token may do.", "OAuth 2.1 consolidates OAuth 2.0 and its best practices, and PKCE is RFC 7636 (2015).", "E5 walks through the five-step authorization flow (Figure 06).", "https://modelcontextprotocol.io (authorization)", aliases=("OAuth", "PKCE", "scope", "PAT"), ch=(14,))
g("A2A", "protocol", "The Agent-to-Agent protocol lets one agent delegate work to another. An agent publishes an Agent Card (its identity, skills, endpoints, and auth requirements). A client agent sends it a Task, which moves through the states submitted, working, input-required, and completed, and returns Artifacts. A2A delegates work to somebody else's agent, whereas MCP gives tools to your own agent.", "Google published it in April 2025 and donated it to the Linux Foundation in June 2025. Version 0.3 (mid-2025) added signed Agent Cards and gRPC, v1.0 arrived in March 2026, and the protocol joined the Agentic AI Foundation in August 2026.", "E5 sets A2A against MCP, and E9 uses its delegation model.", "https://a2a-protocol.org", aliases=("Agent Card", "Agent-to-Agent", "A2A v1.0"), ch=(14, 18))
g("AP2", "protocol", "The Agent Payments Protocol is Google's extension for payments that an agent initiates. It is built on signed 'mandates' that record what the human authorized the agent to buy.", "Google, September 2025.", "E9 uses it as the example of a contract that a marketplace of agents would need.", "https://ap2-protocol.org", aliases=("mandates",), ch=(18,))
g("OWASP", "protocol", "The Open Worldwide Application Security Project publishes two threat lists the course uses. The OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025 list, codes LLM01–LLM10) covers prompt injection, improper output handling, excessive agency, unbounded consumption, and six more. The Agentic Top 10 (December 2025, codes ASI01–ASI10) does the same for agentic systems.", "", "The two lists are the threat vocabulary of E5, E7, and E11, and the decks cite them by code.", "https://genai.owasp.org", aliases=("OWASP Top 10", "Agentic Top 10", "ASI01", "ASI06", "ASI08", "LLM01", "prompt injection", "tool poisoning", "Injection", "injection"), ch=(14, 20))
g("OpenAPI", "protocol", "OpenAPI (formerly Swagger) is the standard description format for HTTP APIs. Several 2023 tool systems derived their tool schemas from OpenAPI documents, and MCP replaced that practice with its own tool listing.", "", "It is in the course as the format that MCP's tool listing replaced.", "https://www.openapis.org", aliases=("REST", "HTTP"))

# ---------------- systems, frameworks, and companies ----------------
g("FIPA", "system", "The Foundation for Intelligent Physical Agents was a 1996 consortium that standardized multi-agent systems. Its agent communication language requires every message to carry a performative (inform, request, propose, refuse), and its interaction protocols include the contract net. FIPA became an IEEE Computer Society standards committee in 2005, and the IEEE and archived copies now host the specifications rather than fipa.org.", "FIPA was founded in 1996 and has been part of the IEEE since 2005.", "E8 takes FIPA as its baseline: a message type is a claim type, and FIPA had that thirty years before the chat transcript.", "https://www.fipa.org", aliases=("performative", "performatives", "FIPA-ACL"), ch=(4,))
g("Contract net", "system", "The contract net is Reid Smith's 1980 protocol for distributing tasks among agents. A manager announces a task, contractors bid, the manager awards the task, and the contractor reports a result or a failure. It is the first plan-do-check protocol with typed messages.", "Smith, 'The Contract Net Protocol' (IEEE Transactions on Computers, 1980).", "It is the first row of E8's 'under every name' table.", "Smith (1980)", aliases=("Contract net (Smith; FIPA)", "contract net"), ch=(4,))
g("BDI", "system", "Belief–Desire–Intention is an agent architecture in which an agent holds beliefs about the world, desires (its goals), and intentions (the plans it has committed to), and reconsiders its intentions when its beliefs change. Bratman's philosophy of intention (1987) became an agent architecture in Rao and Georgeff's work.", "Rao & Georgeff, 'BDI Agents: From Theory to Practice' (ICMAS-95).", "The course reads BDI for what LLM agents carried forward (goal decomposition, and halting as 'intention achieved or dropped') and for what they dropped (an inspectable belief state).", "Rao & Georgeff (1995)", aliases=("Belief–Desire–Intention", "BDI agents"), ch=(4,))
g("PEAS", "system", "PEAS stands for Performance measure, Environment, Actuators, and Sensors. It is Russell and Norvig's frame for specifying an agent's task before designing the agent.", "Russell & Norvig, Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach.", "Chapter 2 starts from PEAS and ends at the nine components.", "Russell & Norvig, AIMA", aliases=("Russell", "Norvig", "AIMA"), ch=(2,))
g("ReAct", "system", "ReAct (Reasoning and Acting) is a prompting pattern in which the model alternates a thought, an action (a tool call), and an observation (the tool's result) until it answers. It was the first widely copied form of the agent loop.", "Yao et al., 'ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models' (2022; ICLR 2023).", "E1's loop is ReAct with a halt condition and a ledger added.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629", aliases=("Yao",), ch=(5,))
g("Chain-of-thought", "system", "Chain-of-thought prompting asks a model to write intermediate reasoning steps before its answer, which improves multi-step accuracy on large models. Tree of Thoughts (2023) generalizes the single chain to a searched tree.", "Wei et al. (2022); Yao et al., 'Tree of Thoughts' (2023).", "E1 presents it as the step from prompting to programs.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903", aliases=("CoT", "Tree of Thoughts", "ToT", "chain-of-thought"), ch=(5,))
g("Toolformer", "system", "Toolformer is a 2023 Meta paper in which a model teaches itself when to call tools (a calculator, search, a calendar). The model inserts API calls into text and keeps the calls that reduce loss.", "Schick et al. (2023).", "It is one of the 2023 'tools' systems in Chapter 6.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761", ch=(6,))
g("Reflexion", "system", "Reflexion is an agent that keeps verbal self-reflections on its failed attempts in an episodic memory and reads them on the next attempt. In the course's terms, each stored reflection is a claim about what went wrong.", "Shinn et al. (2023).", "E7's survey of memory designs covers it.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11366", ch=(6, 16))
g("Generative Agents", "system", "Generative Agents is the 2023 Stanford 'Smallville' paper. Twenty-five agents live in a simulated town with a memory stream, retrieval scored by recency, importance, and relevance, and periodic reflection. It is the reference design for retrieval-scored memory.", "Park et al. (2023).", "Figure 08's pipeline (chunk, represent, store, score, inject) is this paper's pipeline.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442", aliases=("Smallville", "Park"), ch=(6, 16))
g("MemGPT / Letta", "system", "MemGPT (2023) treats the context window like RAM and an external store like disk, and the model pages memory in and out through tools. Letta is the company and the framework that grew out of the paper.", "Packer et al. (2023).", "E7's survey uses it as the case of memory management treated as an operating-system problem.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08560", aliases=("MemGPT", "Letta"), ch=(16,))
g("RAG", "system", "Retrieval-Augmented Generation retrieves the documents relevant to a query, puts them in the prompt, and generates from them. The 2020 paper gave the pattern its name.", "Lewis et al. (2020).", "Every memory layer in the course is a RAG loop over its own records.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401", aliases=("Retrieval-Augmented Generation", "embedding", "embeddings", "embeddinggemma"), ch=(16,))
g("AutoGPT", "system", "AutoGPT is the open-source project of March 2023 that ran GPT-4 in a loop with a goal, tools, and a file-based memory, and went viral. It had autonomy without a halt condition, so it looped, spent, and rarely finished.", "Toran Bruce Richards, 2023.", "E1 uses it as the example of the loop without a halt.", "https://github.com/Significant-Gravitas/AutoGPT", ch=(6,))
g("BabyAGI", "system", "BabyAGI is Yohei Nakajima's task-management loop of March 2023. The loop creates tasks, prioritizes them, executes them, and repeats. Its few hundred lines defined the 'autonomous agent' shape of 2023.", "Yohei Nakajima, March 2023.", "Chapter 6 reads it beside AutoGPT as the 2023 loop in its smallest form.", "https://github.com/yoheinakajima/babyagi", ch=(6,))
g("CAMEL", "system", "CAMEL (Communicative Agents for 'Mind' Exploration) has two role-playing models, an AI user and an AI assistant, solve a task by chatting, with inception prompts that fix their roles. There is no checker, and the assistant ends the task by emitting a token.", "Li et al. (NeurIPS 2023).", "E8 uses it as the example of roles assigned by prompt alone.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.17760", ch=(6, 17))
g("MetaGPT", "system", "MetaGPT is a multi-agent framework in which roles (product manager, architect, engineer, QA) pass structured documents (a PRD, a design, code) to one another, with executable feedback from running the code. The roles are connected by documents rather than by chat.", "Hong et al. (2023; ICLR 2024).", "E8 presents it as the step past CAMEL, with documents between the roles instead of chat.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.00352", ch=(6, 17))
g("AutoGen", "system", "AutoGen is Microsoft's multi-agent conversation framework. An assistant agent and a user-proxy agent that runs code converse until one of them says TERMINATE.", "Wu et al. (2023).", "It is in E8's survey as the framework in which roles are configuration rather than prompts.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08155", ch=(6, 17))
g("CrewAI", "system", "CrewAI is a Python framework in which each agent is defined by a role, a goal, and a backstory, and agents are organized into crews that run a sequential or a hierarchical process. In the hierarchical mode a manager agent reviews the work, and a task guardrail can return (bool, value).", "João Moura, 2023–24.", "It is in Chapter 17's survey of coordination frameworks.", "https://docs.crewai.com", ch=(17,))
g("LangChain", "system", "LangChain is the 2022 library that packaged chains, tools, agents, and memory for LLM applications. LangGraph (2024) is its state-graph runtime, with nodes, edges, conditional routing, a checkpointer, and interrupt() for a human in the loop. LangSmith is its tracing and evaluation service.", "Harrison Chase, October 2022; LangGraph 2024.", "E2 argues that every framework is a theory of who owns the loop, and E8 uses LangGraph's supervisor pattern as a state graph with a human interrupt.", "https://www.langchain.com", aliases=("LangGraph", "LangSmith", "supervisor", "StateGraph", "interrupt()", "checkpointer"), ch=(11, 17))
g("Pydantic AI", "system", "Pydantic AI is a Python agent framework from the Pydantic team, with typed tool definitions, structured outputs validated by Pydantic models, and dependency injection.", "The Pydantic team, 2024 onward.", "The course's airline-operations testbed is built on it.", "https://pydantic.dev/docs/ai/overview/", ch=(11,))
g("Ollama", "system", "Ollama is a local model runner. It pulls open-weight models and serves them over an HTTP API on your machine, including an embeddings endpoint.", "", "It is Route B's substrate, and E3 and E7 require a local model on both routes.", "https://ollama.com", aliases=("local model", "open-weight"))
g("Langfuse", "system", "Langfuse is an open-source observability platform for LLM applications. It records traces, spans, costs, and evaluation runs.", "", "E11's examples of 'receipts over narrative' use trace waterfalls of this kind (Figure 12).", "https://langfuse.com", aliases=("trace", "traces", "span", "spans"), ch=(20,))
g("Anthropic", "org", "Anthropic is the company that makes Claude, Claude Code, and MCP. The course reads its engineering posts: 'Building Effective Agents' (December 2024), 'How we built our multi-agent research system' (June 2025), 'Effective context engineering for AI agents' (September 2025), 'Effective harnesses for long-running agents' (November 2025), the Agent Skills post (October 2025), and 'Demystifying evals for AI agents' (January 2026).", "", "E10 uses the vocabulary of the evals post (task, trial, grader, transcript, outcome).", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering", aliases=("Building Effective Agents", "multi-agent research system", "Claude"))
g("OpenAI", "org", "OpenAI makes the GPT models, ChatGPT, function calling (June 2023), the Agents SDK, and Codex.", "", "Its 'A Practical Guide to Building Agents' (2025) is on the reading list.", "https://openai.com", aliases=("GPT-4", "ChatGPT", "o-series"))
g("Cognition", "org", "Cognition is the company behind Devin. Walden Yan's June 2025 post 'Don't Build Multi-Agents' argues that split workers make conflicting implicit decisions unless they share full traces. It is the published counter-argument to Anthropic's multi-agent research post of the same week.", "", "E8 weighs the two posts against each other.", "https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents", aliases=("Yan", "Devin"), ch=(17,))
g("Invariant Labs", "org", "Invariant Labs is the security group whose April 2025 'tool poisoning' demonstration hid instructions inside an MCP tool description, and the model followed them.", "", "The E5 lab reproduces the demonstration with a harmless canary.", "https://invariantlabs.ai", aliases=("canary",), ch=(14,))
g("Linux Foundation", "org", "The Linux Foundation is home to the Agentic AI Foundation, formed in December 2025 with MCP, goose, and AGENTS.md as founding projects. A2A was donated to the Linux Foundation in June 2025 and joined the Agentic AI Foundation in August 2026.", "", "Both protocols now sit under the foundation, so neither is governed by one vendor.", "https://www.linuxfoundation.org", aliases=("Agentic AI Foundation",))

# ---------------- benchmarks and evaluation ----------------
g("SWE-bench", "bench", "SWE-bench is a benchmark of 2,294 real GitHub issues from twelve Python repositories, on which an agent must produce a patch that passes the repository's tests. SWE-bench Verified (2024) is a human-validated subset of 500 tasks, now saturated at the frontier, and SWE-bench Pro (2025) is the harder successor. SWE-agent (2024) built the first strong agent-computer interface for the benchmark, and Agentless (2024) showed a pipeline with no agent competing with it.", "Jimenez et al. (2023); Yang et al. (2024); Xia et al. (2024).", "E10 asks what five agent benchmarks measure and what they hide, and this is the first of the five.", "https://www.swebench.com", aliases=("SWE-agent", "Agentless", "SWE-bench Verified", "SWE-bench Pro"), ch=(7, 19))
g("HumanEval", "bench", "HumanEval is OpenAI's 2021 set of 164 hand-written Python programming problems with unit tests, scored by pass@k. It was the first standard code benchmark and is now largely saturated.", "Chen et al. (2021).", "It is one of the five benchmarks E10 reads.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03374", aliases=("pass@k",), ch=(19,))
g("GAIA", "bench", "GAIA is a benchmark of real-world assistant questions that need browsing, tool use, and multi-step reasoning, each with an unambiguous answer. The questions are easy for humans and hard for agents.", "Mialon et al. (2023).", "It is in E10's set of five benchmarks.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12983", ch=(19,))
g("WebArena", "bench", "WebArena is a benchmark of tasks for web agents on self-hosted realistic websites (shopping, forums, code hosting).", "Zhou et al. (2023).", "E10 covers it among the five agent benchmarks.", "https://webarena.dev", ch=(19,))
g("ToolBench", "bench", "ToolBench is the dataset from ToolLLM ('Facilitating LLMs to Master 16000+ Real-world APIs', 2023), a benchmark for tool use over thousands of real APIs. Gorilla (2023) is the related Berkeley work on models that call APIs correctly.", "Qin et al. (2023); Patil et al. (2023).", "E10 reads it with the other four agent benchmarks.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.16789", aliases=("Gorilla",), ch=(19,))
g("MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena", "bench", "MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena are LMSYS's 2023 evaluation work. MT-Bench is a set of multi-turn questions graded by a strong model, and Chatbot Arena collects crowd-sourced pairwise preferences. The paper gave LLM-as-a-judge its name and measured three of its biases: position, verbosity, and self-enhancement.", "Zheng et al., 'Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena' (NeurIPS 2023).", "E10's judge-bias table starts with this paper.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685", aliases=("MT-Bench", "Chatbot Arena", "Zheng", "LLM-as-a-judge", "judge", "Judge"), ch=(19,))
g("Judge biases", "bench", "Judge biases are the errors a model makes when it grades another model. They include position bias (Wang et al. 2023, 'Large Language Models are not Fair Evaluators'), verbosity bias, self-preference (Panickssery, Bowman & Feng 2024 showed that models recognize their own outputs, and Wataoka et al. 2024 argue that the mechanism is familiarity), and injection (hidden text in an answer moves the score).", "", "E10's rule follows from these biases: a judge is admissible only if it comes from another model family and survives an injection.", "Wang et al. (2023) arXiv:2305.17926; Panickssery, Bowman & Feng (2024) https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13076; Wataoka et al. (2024) arXiv:2410.21819", aliases=("Panickssery", "Wataoka", "self-preference", "position bias", "verbosity bias"), ch=(19,))
g("BLEU", "bench", "BLEU is the 2002 machine-translation metric that scores n-gram overlap between a candidate translation and the reference translations.", "Papineni et al. (2002).", "The 2017 Transformer paper reports its results in BLEU, and the mini lecture reads those tables.", "https://aclanthology.org/P02-1040/", ch=())
g("MAST", "paper", "MAST is the Multi-Agent System Failure Taxonomy from 'Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail?'. It lists fourteen failure modes in three categories (specification and system design, inter-agent misalignment, and task verification and termination), with prevalence figures from annotated traces.", "Cemri et al. (2025).", "It is E9's taxonomy, and Figure 10's failure matrix is built on it.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657", aliases=("Cemri", "Why Do Multi-Agent LLM Systems Fail"), ch=(18,))
g("Berkeley benchmark exploits", "paper", "The Berkeley benchmark exploits are a 2026 study in which the Berkeley RDI group broke agent benchmarks. Each exploit is a grader that trusted something the agent could touch (the answer file, the judge's prompt, the test harness). The blog post (April 2026) broke eight benchmarks, and the paper, 'Do Androids Dream of Breaking the Game? Systematically Auditing AI Agent Benchmarks with BenchJack' (May 2026), audits ten and reports 219 flaws.", "Wang, Mang, Cheung, Sen & Song, Berkeley RDI blog, April 2026; Wang, Mang, Cheung, Li, Sen & Song, arXiv:2605.12673, May 2026.", "The lesson for your own evals is to check that the agent cannot reach the answers.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12673", aliases=("Berkeley RDI", "BenchJack"), ch=(19,))
g("Transformer", "paper", "The Transformer is the 2017 architecture from 'Attention Is All You Need'. It replaces recurrence with scaled dot-product attention over queries, keys, and values (softmax of QK^T/sqrt(d) times V), uses multi-head attention, and stacks an encoder and a decoder with residual connections and layer normalization. Positions enter through sinusoidal encodings. The authors trained the model with Adam, dropout, and label smoothing, and they report its results in BLEU on the WMT newstest sets. Every model the course's harnesses call descends from it, and GPT (Radford et al., 2018) kept the decoder only.", "Vaswani et al. (2017).", "The mini lecture reads the paper as a design document.", "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762", aliases=("Attention Is All You Need", "Vaswani", "attention", "softmax", "multi-head attention", "decoder-only"), ch=(1,))
g("Degani and Wiener", "paper", "Degani and Wiener's 'Philosophy, policies, procedures and practices: the four P's of flight-deck operations' argues that a procedure is only as good as the practice it produces, and that accidents occur in the gap between the two.", "Asaf Degani and Earl Wiener, chapter in Johnston, McDonald & Fuller (eds.), Aviation Psychology in Practice (Avebury, 1994; Routledge reissue 1997); the same framework is in NASA CR-177642, 'On the design of flight-deck procedures' (1994). Their 1997 IEEE paper, 'Procedures in complex systems: the airline cockpit,' is a separate work.", "The course's 'presence is not procedure' comes from this paper.", "https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19940029437", ch=(3,))
g("Nygard, Release It!", "paper", "Release It! is Michael Nygard's book on production software. It covers stability patterns (the circuit breaker, the bulkhead, timeouts), capacity, and operations. Nygard also wrote the ADR template the course uses.", "Release It! 2nd edition (2018).", "E12's circuit-breaker simulator and its rollback doctrine come from the book.", "Reading list, Appendix E", aliases=("Nygard", "circuit breaker"), ch=(21,))

# ---------------- methods and general terms ----------------
